${macro.generated_by(self.path)}

=[ Catasto ]

Filesystem schema, manifest, and lockfile toolchain (`fs-catasto`).

==[ Overview ]

`catasto` defines and verifies filesystem schemas, directory rules, naming conventions, media manifests, and content lockfiles (SHA-256 and provenance) across a workspace.

Just as a cadastre (*catasto*) records the boundaries, parcels, and ownership of land, `catasto` keeps a repository's directory tree reproducible and governed by explicit rules.

==[ Usage ]

```bash
catasto init                                    # create catasto.ctmt
catasto upstream add NAME GIT [--rev R] [--root DIR]   # name a place files come from
catasto add DEST --upstream NAME PATH           # take a file: declare it, fetch it, lock it
catasto add DEST --git URL [--rev R] PATH       #   ... from a repository written out
catasto add DEST --local PATH                   #   ... from a file on disk
catasto list [--json]                           # every declared file, its source and its state
catasto remove DEST [--delete]                  # stop managing a file
catasto upstream remove NAME                    # drop a name nothing uses
catasto upstream list
catasto check [path]                            # report drift between catasto.ctmt, catasto.lock, and the files
catasto sync [--update] [--only FILE]... [path] # fetch managed files from their sources, then lock
catasto lock [path]                             # record the sha256 of every managed file in catasto.lock
```

Every command works on the current directory; `-C DIR` (`--workspace DIR`) says another. `check`, `lock` and `sync` also take the directory as a trailing path, as `tomet check` and `twrit check` do.

==[ Getting Started ]

```bash
catasto init
catasto upstream add vocabularies https://github.com/tomet-lang/vocabularies.git --root vocabularies
catasto add tmtroot/schema/vocabularies/writ@1.0.0.vocabulary.tmt --upstream vocabularies writ/writ@1.0.0.vocabulary.tmt
catasto check
```

That writes `catasto.ctmt` and `catasto.lock`, and the file itself. Commit the three. A fresh clone has the file already; `catasto check` says whether it is as locked, and `catasto sync` brings it back if not.

`add` declares the file and reads it from the place, at the commit its `rev` points at now; the other files and what the lock says about them stay as they are. If the file cannot be read, the manifest is put back as it was. `remove` forgets the declaration and the lock entry, and leaves the file on disk unless `--delete`.

The commands edit `catasto.ctmt` without rewriting it: comments, order and layout stay, a new entry goes after the last, and the result is read again before it is saved, so an edit that would leave a manifest that does not load is refused. You can also write the file by hand, as shown under Files.

`catasto list` shows `name:path` for a file taken from a named place, and `git URL@REV:path` or `local PATH` for the others, with `ok` or the way it has drifted, and the commit it was locked at. `--json` is the same for tools.

`catasto sync --only FILE` reads one file again. With `--update` it moves that file to where its `rev` points now and nothing else, which is how to take one new vocabulary without taking all of them.

`check` exits with 1 when it finds drift, and never touches the network. It names the command that puts each file right. Files that are neither declared nor locked are unmanaged and never reported.

The names are exact: `catasto.ctmt` and `catasto.lock`, and `init` takes no other. A workspace that has `Catasto.ctmt` or `CATASTO.lock` instead is told to rename it, rather than being read as one with nothing declared or locked, which is what a case-sensitive filesystem would otherwise make of it.

==[ Files ]

`catasto.ctmt` is written by hand, in cotomet, the data language of the Tomet ecosystem. It maps each managed path to what is known about it, and today that is where its content comes from.

```text
"docs/shared/terms.tmt": { source: "../shared/terms.tmt" }
```

The key is a path relative to the workspace and must stay inside it. Quote a path that is not a bare word, which is any path with a `/` or a space in it. An entry is always a map, and a path may be declared once.

A `source` is one of three kinds:

-| `"..."` -- a file on the local filesystem, relative to the workspace root. It may point outside the workspace.
-| `{ git: "...", rev: "...", path: "..." }` -- a file in a git repository. `git` is an `https://` URL or a local path (relative to the workspace root), `rev` is a tag, a branch, or a commit already reachable from one, and `path` is the file's location inside the repository. Quote a `rev` that reads as a number (`rev: "1.2"`). An optional `symlink: keep` preserves the symlink in the workspace rather than following it to write the target file content.
-| `{ upstream: name, path: "..." }` -- the same, with the repository and `rev` named once in `::upstreams` (below).

```text
"tmtroot/schema/vocabularies/rust@1.0.0.vocabulary.tmt": {
  source: { git: "https://github.com/tomet-lang/vocabularies.git", rev: main, path: "vocabularies/rust/rust@1.0.0.vocabulary.tmt" }
}
```

==[ Upstreams ]

Files that come from the same place name it once. `::upstreams` goes at the top of `catasto.ctmt`, before the first path, as a map from a name to `git` and `rev`, and optionally a `root`:

```text
::upstreams{
  vocabularies: { git: "https://github.com/tomet-lang/vocabularies.git", rev: main, root: vocabularies }
}

"tmtroot/schema/vocabularies/rust@1.0.0.vocabulary.tmt": {
  source: { upstream: vocabularies, path: "rust/rust@1.0.0.vocabulary.tmt" }
}
```

`root` is the directory of the repository that the `path`s of its entries start from: above, `rust/rust@1.0.0.vocabulary.tmt` is the file `vocabularies/rust/rust@1.0.0.vocabulary.tmt`. Without it they start at the top of the repository. It must be relative and stay inside the repository, and a `path` cannot climb out of it.

An upstream is not a macro and the name does not leave the manifest. It is read into the full `git`, `rev` and `path` while the file is loaded, so `catasto.lock` records the same `git`, `rev`, `commit` and `sha256` as when they are written out, and moving an entry between the two forms does not make it stale. The `git` and `rev` of an upstream are checked where it is declared. An entry says `upstream` or `git` and `rev`, not both. `::upstreams` appears once, and it is the only declaration catasto reads.

`catasto.lock` is generated by `catasto lock` and `catasto sync`. Commit it together with the managed files, so a fresh clone can run `catasto check` without the sources. It is JSON, keyed by path under `files`, with a `version`; nobody edits it by hand.

==[ Git Sources ]

Git sources are read through the system `git`, restricted to the `https` and `file` transports. ssh is not supported on purpose, so catasto never depends on keys, agents or host aliases: `git@host:path`, `ssh://`, `git://` and `http://` are rejected when the manifest is loaded. Each URL gets one bare clone in `$CATASTO_CACHE_DIR`, else `$XDG_CACHE_HOME/catasto`, else `~/.cache/catasto`. Nothing is written into the workspace besides the managed files and `catasto.lock`.

`catasto sync` reads different URLs at the same time and the files of one URL one after another, since a URL has one clone to update. How many run at once is the jobserver's to say: the one a parent such as `make`, `cargo` or `sisho` passes down in `MAKEFLAGS`, else one per available CPU. The result does not depend on which URL answers first: files are written in the order of `catasto.ctmt`, and when several sources fail the error reported is the first of them in that order. The Nix package puts a `git` after the one on `PATH`, so `sync` works where none is installed.

The lock records the commit that `rev` resolved to. Like `cargo build`, `catasto sync` reproduces that commit, restoring a modified file to it, and only touches the network when the commit is not cached yet. A source whose declaration changed is resolved again. `catasto sync --update` resolves every `rev` again, like `cargo update`.

`catasto lock` never resolves a revision, so it cannot pin a git source that has no recorded commit. Run `catasto sync` for that.

When `path` points to a symlink in the repository, catasto follows it by default (up to 8 hops) and writes the resolved target file's content as a regular file. Symlinks pointing to an empty target, containing NUL bytes, absolute paths, or escaping the repository root are refused. Intermediate directory components may not be symlinks. With `symlink: keep`, the upstream entry must be a symlink, and catasto places the symlink itself into the workspace with its exact target text intact. Writes to the destination never write through an existing symlink, replacing it atomically instead.

==[ Drift ]

-| `modified` -- the file differs from its locked sha256, or its file type (regular file vs symlink) does not match the declaration.
-| `missing` -- the file does not exist.
-| `stale` -- the source differs from the locked sha256. Reported only for local sources that are readable; a git source is never consulted.
-| `unlocked` -- declared, but the lock has no entry, records another source or symlink mode, or lacks the commit of a git source.
-| `orphaned` -- locked, but no longer declared.

==[ Workspace Layout ]

-| @dir(app) (`catasto-cli`) -- Main CLI binary (`catasto`). Layer 3.
-| @dir(crates/catasto-core) (`catasto-core`) -- Core library for filesystem schema parsing, directory rule evaluation, and lockfile verification. Layer 2.
-| @dir(crates/catasto-git) (`catasto-git`) -- Reads files out of git repositories through the system `git`, in a cache of bare clones. Layer 1.
-| @dir(crates/catasto-digest) (`catasto-digest`) -- SHA-256 content hashing. Layer 0.

==[ Building and Testing ]

=== Build workspace
```bash
cargo build --workspace
```

=== Run tests
```bash
cargo test --workspace
```

=== Check workspace
```bash
cargo run -p catasto-cli -- check .
```
